  "legacy:2021-148-309cfd3f0d": {
    "title": {
      "source": "侘寂之美",
      "en": "The Beauty of Wabi-sabi"
    },
    "caption": {
      "source": "侘（chà）寂源自小乘佛法中的三法印：诸行无常、诸法无我、涅槃寂静。不少人认为，它是东方美学的最高境界，看似粗糙，却体现出无与伦比的绝妙质感。\n\n在日本发展盛行之后，它逐渐成为了一类典型的日式美学。其核心要素便是残破、不规则、不对称。一言蔽之，这是一种“以不完美为美”、“大成若缺”的美学概念。\n\n这种概念时刻提醒着我们世界与宇宙的宏大，以及人类的渺小。在无常的变化中找到并接受残缺的美感，也许能让我们更好地与自然和社会共处。\n\n自从业开始，Belongs的作品便深受这种美学的影响。所以我们穷尽所能，争取在作品中呈现出更丰富、更真实的手工感。因为我们坚信，这样的作品才具有长久而鲜活的生命力。\n\n我们的工作会以侘寂美学的概念为出发点。\n\n配制金银时加入不同原料，是为了追求独有的表面质感。\n\n旧化处理采用独家手法，同样是为了塑造岁月斑驳的效果。\n\n作品中的细小裂纹、破损，毫无疑问，亦是刻意营造出的专属细节。\n\n大家通常会深深着迷于古老银饰的氧化质感和残破表面（也就是俗话中的“老味”），其实这也正是侘寂的完美体现。\n\n我们对此所做的一切努力，正是为了让大家尽早获得与老物类似的体验。试想在长期把玩、佩戴之后，各位心爱的银饰又会是怎样一番景象！\n\n值得一提，侘寂和粗制滥造是完全的两极。侘寂是以极致精细、无比繁复的手法来刻意表达的残缺之美，绝非偷工减料滥竽充数的粗糙制作。",
      "en": "Wabi-sabi comes from the three seals of Hinayana Buddhism: impermanence, non-self and the stillness of nirvana. Many regard it as the highest form of Eastern aesthetics: seemingly rough, yet with an unparalleled beauty of texture.\n\nAs it developed and flourished in Japan, it became a characteristic Japanese aesthetic. Its central elements are wear, irregularity and asymmetry: finding beauty in imperfection, and a sense of completeness in what appears incomplete.\n\nThis idea reminds us of the vastness of the world and universe, and how small we are. Finding and accepting imperfect beauty amid change may help us live more comfortably with nature and society.\n\nSince the start of our practice, this aesthetic has deeply influenced Belongs. We do all we can to give the pieces a richer, more honest handmade character, believing this is what gives them enduring vitality.\n\nWabi-sabi is a starting point for our work.\n\nWe add different ingredients when preparing gold and silver alloys to pursue distinctive surface textures.\n\nWe use our own aging methods to create the mottled effect of passing years.\n\nThe small cracks and worn areas in the pieces are also deliberately created, individual details.\n\nPeople are often drawn to the oxidized textures and worn surfaces of old silver, its sense of age. This, too, embodies wabi-sabi.\n\nAll this effort aims to offer an experience like that of an old object sooner. Imagine how your beloved silver will look after years of handling and wearing.\n\nIt is worth stressing that wabi-sabi and shoddy workmanship are polar opposites. Wabi-sabi deliberately expresses imperfect beauty through meticulous, intricate methods. It is not careless work, corner-cutting or inferior making."
    },
    "heading": {
      "source": "侘寂之美",
      "en": "The Beauty of Wabi-sabi"
    },
    "lead": {
      "source": "",
      "en": ""
    }
  },
